<description>
Fill black gaps by propagating the maximally supported repeating translation.

Input structure
A same-size colored periodic field contains black pixels that mark missing cells.
All non-black pixels are already correct and provide many repeated-color pairs.

Rule
Find the nonzero translation vector whose overlapping visible cells have the
largest support with no color disagreements.  Cells connected by this vector
and its inverse are one periodic equivalence class; replace each black cell by
the unique visible color in its class.

Key insight
The occlusions hide a repeated lattice, and the strongest self-translation of
the visible pixels identifies exactly how to copy colors through the gaps.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "non-black pixels of a periodic color field with black missing-cell masks",
  "transform": "infer the maximally supported self-translation whose overlapping visible pixels agree, then fill every black pixel from the unique color in its translation-equivalence class"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 29x29 (input and output same size)
  output_pattern:
    description: 2D periodic color field tiling the grid, no black pixels
    lattice_vectors: two oblique generators v1 (shorter) and v2 (longer)
    v1_norm: 2.8-6.7 (strictly shortest lattice vector)
    v2_norm: 4.5-9.0 (strictly longer than v1)
    tile_area: 12-54 (|det(v1,v2)|)
    n_colors: 5-7 (non-black, drawn from 1-9)
    each equivalence class under v1 alone has exactly one color
  black_mask:
    description: 2-4 connected black blobs erasing parts of the periodic pattern
    total_coverage: 7-13% of pixels
    n_components: 2-4 (4-connected)
    component_size: 6-57 pixels each
    shape: roughly rectangular or L-shaped blobs, may touch grid edge [observed]
  solver_constraints:
    v1_has_unique_max_support: strictly higher support count than any other translation
    every_equivalence_class_visible: each class under v1 retains >=1 non-black pixel

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose two lattice vectors v1, v2 with |v1| < |v2| and tile_area 12-54
    2. Build a 29x29 periodic color field by assigning a random non-black color (from 5-7 chosen colors) to each of the tile_area equivalence classes of the full 2D lattice
    3. Ensure each chain under v1-only also has a single color (automatic since v1-chains are subsets of 2D-lattice classes)
    4. Verify v1 is the unique shortest lattice vector (check v2, v1+v2, v1-v2 all longer)
    5. Place 2-4 connected rectangular or L-shaped black blobs (total 7-13% of pixels), scattered across the grid
    6. Verify every v1-equivalence class retains >=1 visible pixel; if not, shrink or shift blobs
    7. Verify max-support translation on the masked grid yields exactly {v1, -v1} (reject otherwise)
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

